<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Where's the Puddle? – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's the Rainy Season</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Where's the Puddle?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Relay the teacher's instructions during group activities. concept: Explain steps in an experiment to peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can relay teacher's instructions about rainy day activities using basic gestures (e.g., points to clouds and says, 'Draw clouds.'). Can explain steps in an experiment by demonstrating actions (e.g., shows pouring water for 'This is rain.'). Can help peers understand rainy season concepts by using simple words and gestures (e.g., pointing to rain and saying, 'Rain is water.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 35%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 15%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and asks, "What's the weather like today?" (If rainy, "It is raining!"). The teacher then shows flashcards of rainy season items (e.g., umbrella, raincoat, boots, puddle, cloud, storm). The teacher points to each item and asks, "What is this?" encouraging students to recall vocabulary from previous lessons. Students say the word. The teacher then gives a simple instruction related to an item, e.g., "Point to the umbrella!" or "Show me the boots!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to relay a simple instruction using gestures and words. For example, the teacher silently shows a flashcard of 'cloud' and whispers an instruction to Student A, "Draw a cloud." Student A then turns to Student B and relays, "Draw a cloud," using gestures (e.g., pointing to the sky, making a cloud shape with hands). The teacher then models explaining a simple action: "This is a puddle. When it rains, we splash in puddles." (Teacher demonstrates splashing action). The teacher emphasizes using simple words and clear gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of sandals: "Do we wear sandals in the rain?" (Expected: No!) "What do we wear?" (Expected: Boots!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher makes a pouring motion: "What am I doing?" (Expected: Pouring!) "What do we pour?" (Expected: Water!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's the Rainy Season")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated set of instructions for a simple "DIY Rain Experiment" or a simplified weather report script (genre: instructions/descriptive dialogue; register: informal; format: illustrated steps/short sentences).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text/Instructions:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"Hello! Today, it is rainy and windy. I need an umbrella and a raincoat. I wear my boots. What do you wear in the rain?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"Let's make rain! You need a cup and water. First, pour water into the cup. Next, drop water with your finger. This is rain! Is it wet?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Talking about rainy weather: 'I need an umbrella.' Sharing experiences: 'I wear my boots when it rains.' Narrating current events related to weather: Using the present continuous tense (e.g., 'It is raining. I like puddle-jumping.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gist question: "What is the weather like?" (Expected: Rainy and windy!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: "The person needs sandals for the rain." (Expected: False!) "The person needs an umbrella." (Expected: True!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Information matching: Students match pictures of items (umbrella, boots) to sentences from the text ("I need an umbrella.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Relay teacher's instructions, explain simple steps. Grammar: Present simple (What do you need?), Present continuous (It is raining.), WH-questions (What do you wear?). Vocabulary: puddle, umbrella, raincoat, boots, splash, pour, wet, rainy, muddy, sky, cloud.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled practice (Relay Game):</b> Students sit in small groups. The teacher whispers a short instruction (e.g., "Draw a rainy sky with a cloud.") to the first student in each group. That student then relays the instruction to the next student using simple words and gestures. The last student draws what they understood. The teacher checks if the drawing matches the original instruction, focusing on clear relaying. Pronunciation focus: /r/ in 'rain' and /p/ in 'puddle'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled (Show and Explain):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has 3-4 flashcards of rainy season items (e.g., 'boots', 'raincoat', 'umbrella', 'puddle'). Student A picks a card (e.g., 'boots'). Student B's task is to *explain* to Student C (imaginary or another peer) what it is and what it's for in the rain, using sentences like, "This is boots. I wear them when it rains." or "They protect my feet from the muddy puddle." Student B must use gestures to help explain.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair interaction/peer check (Experiment Explanation):</b> Students are given simple illustrated instructions for the "DIY Rain Experiment" from Stage 2. In pairs, Student A reads step 1, then Student B must *explain and demonstrate* that step to Student C (their partner or another imaginary person) using simple phrases like, "First, pour water," and mimicking the action. They take turns explaining and demonstrating each step. The focus is on clarity and using action verbs like 'pour' and 'drop'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students) to *prepare* for the "Weather Report Role-Play" project. Their task is to *mediate* the planning process by explaining ideas and relaying instructions within their group. One student will act as the "group's mediator" who explains their weather report plan to the teacher or another group.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Group Planning & Explanation. Each group creates a simple weather report plan for "It's the Rainy Season." They decide: 1) What the weather is like (e.g., "It is raining! It is windy!"). 2) What they need (e.g., "I need an umbrella and a raincoat."). 3) What they wear (e.g., "I wear my boots."). 4) One simple action they will demonstrate (e.g., "I splash in puddles!"). The mediation focus is on *explaining* these choices to their group members and then *presenting the plan* to the teacher or another group.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme: "Where's the Puddle?" and Scenario: "It's the Rainy Season":</b> Students use the target vocabulary to describe the rainy weather conditions and discuss the necessary items. They must explain *why* they need certain items (e.g., "We need boots because it is muddy outside.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in explaining the steps and content of their weather report. Collaborative interaction in relaying ideas and instructions among group members. Communicative task completion of outlining a simple weather report using target vocabulary and grammar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group planning and presentation of their plan, using a simple checklist for mediation skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group, through one designated "mediator" student, presents their weather report plan to the class. The mediator explains the group's decisions for the weather report (e.g., "Our weather report says, 'It is raining!' We need an umbrella. We wear boots and a raincoat. We will splash in puddles!"). Other group members may add with simple phrases or gestures. The focus is on the mediator's ability to clearly explain the group's plan using words and gestures, and the group's collaborative effort.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary (e.g., 'rain', 'puddle').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (puddle, umbrella, raincoat, boots, splash, wet, rainy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in relaying the group's plan and instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of basic gestures to aid understanding (e.g., pointing, mimicking actions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and participation within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete short reflection prompts.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can explain what I need for a rainy day." (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can help my friends understand rainy day words." (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy when you explained rainy day things to your friends?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult when you tried to help your friends understand?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The teacher considers:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students relay instructions and explain concepts? (e.g., "The relay game effectively encouraged simple word use and gestures.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for students to improve their mediation skills, especially in explaining multi-step processes or abstract concepts? (e.g., "More specific sentence frames for explaining steps, like 'First, you say...' 'Then, you do...' are needed.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the students able to effectively explain their weather report plans using the target language and gestures?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Where's the Puddle?" theme by integrating all skills through mediation and project work. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, applying the general mediation skills of explaining and relaying information to new vocabulary and concepts. The ability to give and follow instructions will be critical for any new theme's activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>